1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,177
Aarav reads the label on his room heater.

2
00:00:03,177 --> 00:00:05,138
Two hundred and thirty volts.

3
00:00:05,488 --> 00:00:09,652
But on a scope, the mains voltage
swings up to about three hundred and

4
00:00:09,652 --> 00:00:13,796
twenty five volts, then down to
minus three hundred and twenty five.

5
00:00:14,146 --> 00:00:16,577
So which number is the real voltage?

6
00:00:17,177 --> 00:00:19,142
It starts with one problem.

7
00:00:19,142 --> 00:00:21,841
An alternating voltage never sits still.

8
00:00:22,191 --> 00:00:25,215
Its highest point is
called the peak value, V m.

9
00:00:25,565 --> 00:00:28,947
From the top to the bottom
is the peak to peak value.

10
00:00:28,947 --> 00:00:30,208
That is two V m.

11
00:00:30,558 --> 00:00:32,585
Now try to find its average.

12
00:00:32,585 --> 00:00:35,793
Every positive half has
a matching negative half.

13
00:00:36,143 --> 00:00:38,507
Over one full cycle, they cancel.

14
00:00:38,507 --> 00:00:39,999
The average is zero.

15
00:00:39,999 --> 00:00:42,673
Yet this voltage clearly heats a heater.

16
00:00:42,673 --> 00:00:44,475
So we need better values.

17
00:00:45,125 --> 00:00:46,948
Let's test it on the breadboard.

18
00:00:47,298 --> 00:00:52,016
Here is a heater coil on an alternating
supply with a peak of ten volts.

19
00:00:52,366 --> 00:00:53,744
Watch the electrons.

20
00:00:53,744 --> 00:00:55,777
They just rock back and forth.

21
00:00:55,777 --> 00:00:57,714
But the coil still gets hot.

22
00:00:58,064 --> 00:01:00,861
Below it, the same coil on a DC battery.

23
00:01:00,861 --> 00:01:02,999
Let's turn the battery up slowly.

24
00:01:03,349 --> 00:01:06,413
At five volts, it makes
less heat than the top coil.

25
00:01:06,763 --> 00:01:10,037
At ten volts, the peak value,
it makes twice as much.

26
00:01:10,387 --> 00:01:15,137
At seven point zero seven volts,
both coils make exactly the same heat

27
00:01:15,137 --> 00:01:16,230
every second.

28
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:22,246
So this alternating voltage is worth
seven point zero seven volts of DC.

29
00:01:22,246 --> 00:01:24,767
That number is its RMS value.

30
00:01:25,417 --> 00:01:29,699
The RMS value is the steady
DC that gives the same heat.

31
00:01:30,499 --> 00:01:32,467
Why seven point zero seven?

32
00:01:32,467 --> 00:01:33,854
Let's work it out.

33
00:01:34,204 --> 00:01:36,181
Heat every second is power.

34
00:01:36,181 --> 00:01:39,292
For a resistor, P equals v squared over R.

35
00:01:39,992 --> 00:01:42,847
Here, v equals V m sine omega t.

36
00:01:42,847 --> 00:01:48,122
So the power is V m squared, times
sine squared omega t, over R.

37
00:01:48,822 --> 00:01:51,131
Squaring makes every value positive.

38
00:01:51,131 --> 00:01:54,411
And on average, sine
squared is exactly one half.

39
00:01:55,111 --> 00:01:58,631
So the average power is
V m squared over two R.

40
00:01:59,331 --> 00:02:02,761
A battery of voltage V
gives V squared over R.

41
00:02:02,761 --> 00:02:04,274
Set the two equal.

42
00:02:04,974 --> 00:02:07,657
V equals V m divided by root two.

43
00:02:07,657 --> 00:02:11,489
That is zero point seven
zero seven times the peak.

44
00:02:12,189 --> 00:02:14,720
Square it, take the mean, then the root.

45
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:17,751
That is why it is called
the root mean square value.

46
00:02:17,751 --> 00:02:19,521
Current follows the same rule.

47
00:02:20,171 --> 00:02:21,891
Now, the average value.

48
00:02:21,891 --> 00:02:26,230
Over a full cycle it is zero, so
we average over one half cycle.

49
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:28,360
Take one hump of the wave.

50
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:32,394
Flatten it into a rectangle with
the same area and the same width.

51
00:02:32,744 --> 00:02:36,178
The height of that rectangle
is the average value.

52
00:02:36,178 --> 00:02:41,094
It is two V m over pi, or zero point
six three seven times the peak.

53
00:02:41,794 --> 00:02:44,332
Two more numbers link these values.

54
00:02:44,332 --> 00:02:47,375
The form factor is RMS divided by average.

55
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:50,600
For a sine wave, it is one point one one.

56
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:54,243
The peak factor is peak divided by RMS.

57
00:02:54,243 --> 00:02:57,922
That is root two, about
one point four one four.

58
00:02:58,572 --> 00:02:59,829
Worked example.

59
00:02:59,829 --> 00:03:01,671
Back to Aarav's charger.

60
00:03:01,671 --> 00:03:05,140
Two hundred and thirty
volts is the RMS value.

61
00:03:05,490 --> 00:03:08,485
The peak is two hundred
and thirty times root two.

62
00:03:08,485 --> 00:03:11,427
That is about three hundred
and twenty five volts.

63
00:03:12,027 --> 00:03:16,563
The half cycle average is zero point
six three seven times the peak.

64
00:03:16,563 --> 00:03:18,928
About two hundred and seven volts.

65
00:03:19,528 --> 00:03:23,237
And peak to peak, it is about
six hundred and fifty volts.

66
00:03:23,237 --> 00:03:24,966
So both numbers were real.

67
00:03:24,966 --> 00:03:27,115
They just measure different things.

68
00:03:27,765 --> 00:03:29,230
Now, the exam trap.

69
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:34,119
Many students write that the average
value of a sine wave is zero point

70
00:03:34,119 --> 00:03:35,703
six three seven V m.

71
00:03:35,703 --> 00:03:38,177
Over a full cycle, that is wrong.

72
00:03:38,527 --> 00:03:41,343
Over a full cycle the average is zero.

73
00:03:41,343 --> 00:03:45,938
Zero point six three seven V m
is the average over half a cycle.

74
00:03:46,588 --> 00:03:48,219
Where is this asked?

75
00:03:48,219 --> 00:03:51,152
In PUC board papers, and in JEE Main.

76
00:03:51,152 --> 00:03:56,328
Convert peak, RMS and average values,
find form factor, or find the heat

77
00:03:56,328 --> 00:03:57,565
in a resistor.

78
00:03:58,215 --> 00:03:59,115
Try this.

79
00:03:59,044 --> 00:04:03,481
A sine wave voltage with a peak of
two hundred volts is applied across

80
00:04:03,481 --> 00:04:05,037
a fifty ohm resistor.

81
00:04:05,037 --> 00:04:07,797
How much heat is produced in one minute?

82
00:04:13,197 --> 00:04:14,097
B.

83
00:04:13,687 --> 00:04:15,578
Twenty four kilojoules.

84
00:04:15,928 --> 00:04:20,210
First, the RMS voltage squared
is the peak squared over two.

85
00:04:20,210 --> 00:04:22,015
That is twenty thousand.

86
00:04:22,365 --> 00:04:25,041
Power is that divided by fifty ohms.

87
00:04:25,041 --> 00:04:26,537
Four hundred watts.

88
00:04:26,887 --> 00:04:28,773
Heat is power times time.

89
00:04:28,773 --> 00:04:33,017
Four hundred times sixty seconds
is twenty four thousand joules.

90
00:04:33,667 --> 00:04:35,189
Screenshot this card.

91
00:04:38,389 --> 00:04:41,309
Next, inductive and capacitive reactance.

92
00:04:41,309 --> 00:04:46,026
Why does a capacitor block DC, but
let alternating current through?
